WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.637 --> 00:00:02.760
- [Reporter] $3 trillion worth of goods

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.788
travels through the
Chicago region every year.

00:00:05.788 --> 00:00:06.621
(train horn honks)

00:00:06.621 --> 00:00:08.790
Much of that is transported
on trains like these,

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.030
making Chicago the busiest
rail hub in the US.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:14.040
- We like to say when Chicago has a cold,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.110
the rest of the nation starts sneezing.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:18.840
Really, because so much
freight moves through Chicago,

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.700
it can have a big impact.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:22.530
- [Reporter] And a
stoppage possibly caused

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:25.290
by a nationwide rail worker
strike or bad weather

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:28.050
would have all those
trains at a standstill.

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.957
Here's why running railroads
are so important to Chicago

00:00:30.957 --> 00:00:32.430
and the national economy

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.923
and the ripple effects
of a potential shutdown.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:36.930
- In the Chicago region,

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:39.330
we have a very multimodal dynamic system.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.950
We have 10 interstate highways.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.380
Six of the seven biggest
railroad companies

00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:45.600
have lines in the Chicago region.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:48.690
- [Reporter] That system pushes
enormous amounts of freight.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:51.600
More than 7.6 million
freight cargo containers

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:53.910
were moved in Chicago in 2021.

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.940
That's up from 6.1 million in 2009.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.620
The area is an important hub

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:01.500
in the intricate nationwide
logistics web that gets goods

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:04.230
all across the country,
and the region's railways

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:07.020
are particularly important
strings in the web.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:08.910
In recent years, the city's rail yards

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:11.040
have been the point of severe bottlenecks

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:13.740
as the supply chain choked up nationally.

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:16.830
Chicago sees about 25%
of all freight trains

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.050
and 50% of all intermodal trains,

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.000
or the ones that carry shipping containers

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:22.800
or trailers in the US.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.350
- Freight that has to go
from one end of the country

00:01:25.350 --> 00:01:28.800
to the other often comes through Chicago

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.310
because it has to be switched
off to another railroad.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:33.180
- [Reporter] That's because six

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.060
of the seven major Class
I North American railroads

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:37.860
converge in Chicago.

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.780
- Railroads began growing

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:42.510
in the latter part of the 19th century.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.180
The East Coast railroads pushed west.

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:47.430
The West Coast railroads pushed east,

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.800
and they met in Chicago.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.990
- [Reporter] The freight rail
industry plays a huge role

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:55.530
in the region's $645 billion economy.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.660
Local transportation and logistics firms

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.800
provided $13.8 billion
in services in 2021.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:04.320
- The diverse economy here
really means that a number

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.450
of the different types of
industries that we have,

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.690
whether it's manufacturing,
whether it's the retail trades,

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:12.150
the construction trades,
the wholesale trades,

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.400
they're all dependent on goods

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.890
being delivered on a daily basis.

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.050
- [Reporter] And over a
quarter of the region's jobs

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.720
are in industries that
rely on cargo transport,

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:24.030
including rail, but it's not just freight

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:26.490
that would be incapacitated
by a rail stoppage.

00:02:26.490 --> 00:02:28.893
Public transit depends
on the railroads, too.

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.040
- In the Chicago region, our Metra system,

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.540
our suburban commuter rail

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:35.880
runs on all of the freight rail lines.

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.430
Those trains, the maintenance of the track

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.370
is also maintained by
the freight rail workers.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:44.010
- [Reporter] So if the trains
ever stopped in Chicago...

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.600
- It's felt nationwide.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:48.120
A railroad strike would
have a significant effect

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:49.590
on the local economy.

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:51.330
- [Reporter] A nationwide
rail worker strike

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.460
averted by Congress in early December

00:02:53.460 --> 00:02:56.340
nearly stilled the
region's bustling railways.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.020
- There was worry in the Chicago region

00:02:58.020 --> 00:02:59.280
about a possible strike.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.190
A lot of our jobs, a lot of our businesses

00:03:02.190 --> 00:03:04.650
are based on that import-export model,

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:08.490
and they would all be impacted
by a potential strike.

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:09.323
- [Reporter] That could mean

00:03:09.323 --> 00:03:11.970
up to 1,300 stopped trains every day.

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.130
About 500 of those are freight trains

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:16.050
and 800 are passenger trains.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:18.330
- 60% of the jobs in the Chicago region

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:19.680
have to be done in person.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:22.650
All of those people, if they
are traditionally relying

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:25.110
on transit and suburban commuter rails

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:26.970
to get into the places
that they need to go,

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:28.170
they just wouldn't be able to,

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:32.370
and our roads can't handle 60% of people

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.620
choosing to drive over taking transit.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.810
- [Reporter] Leading up to a
possible strike in September,

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.320
regional commuter rail Metra
warned of service disruptions.

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.110
But that isn't the only
thing that could stop trains.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:44.430
Bad weather and snowstorms

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.350
have impacted service in the past.

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.500
In 1999, a blizzard shut
down the rail system,

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.570
affecting operations for months.

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:54.270
- You had goods sitting
on the railroad lines

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:57.570
like soybeans and corn
that were just going bad

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.640
and so they weren't even able

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:01.290
to deliver many of the shipments

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:02.700
that were sitting there waiting.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:04.980
- [Reporter] As a result,
the region launched CREATE,

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:07.530
a plan to eliminate rail bottlenecks.

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:10.620
Still, another harsh
winter struck in 2014.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.040
Traffic snarled, sending
delays throughout the system.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.480
- Trains couldn't get in here,

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:17.580
they couldn't get out of Chicago.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.770
The whole system kind of seized up.

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:23.850
There were trains stacked up
kind of all over the country,

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.350
all over North America

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:28.860
waiting to get somewhere
and they couldn't.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.930
- [Reporter] Goods like
cars, coal, and fertilizer

00:04:30.930 --> 00:04:32.940
were all affected by the stoppages.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:36.338
It took warm weather for traffic
to flow efficiently again.

00:04:36.338 --> 00:04:37.470
(train couplers clanking)

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.960
That national gridlock made
it clear that railroads,

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:42.990
including Chicago's, are
vital in the supply chain.

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.590
- Railroads in Chicago help
keep the economy moving.

00:04:46.590 --> 00:04:49.710
It kind of keeps the rest of
the country moving as well.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.530
When the rail system has trouble,

00:04:52.530 --> 00:04:55.500
the rest of that
transportation infrastructure

00:04:55.500 --> 00:04:57.285
suffers as well.

00:04:57.285 --> 00:04:59.962
(bright instrumental music)

